00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.422
NowThis: I can’t breathe. I can’t breathe.

00:00:03.422 --> 00:00:03.488
New York Times: Hands up, don’t&nbsp;
shoot. Hands up, don’t shoot.

00:00:03.488 --> 00:00:03.524
Ruptly: Di Su Nombre, George Floyd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.524 --> 00:00:03.593
Di Su Nombre, George Floyd, Di Su&nbsp;
Nombre,George Floyd, Di Su Nombre.

00:00:03.593 --> 00:00:20.441
TODAY: Overnight, Chaos in Minneapolis.

00:00:20.441 --> 00:00:20.503
Sky News: The biggest gathering&nbsp;
that London has seen in weeks.

00:00:20.503 --> 00:00:30.180
Face The Nation: From Berlin, to Seoul, South&nbsp;
Korea, to Rio in Brazil, to Sydney, Australia.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.920
CBS: Everywhere, George Floyd’s&nbsp;
death has struck a mighty chord.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:43.980
ODUAH: So they're always looking at America&nbsp;
with rose colored glasses or whatever. But now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.240
you know, I think that that view&nbsp;
of America as a beacon of hope and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.320
democracy has been tinted and they are&nbsp;
somewhat disillusioned by this current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.760
administration and they're making&nbsp;
comments to reflect their thoughts.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.960
RICHBURG:
After the murder of George Floyd on television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.640
and it was a murder, I had people walking up to&nbsp;
me or I had students knocking on my door saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.920
"Oh my God, what's happening in America? What's&nbsp;
going on? Can you explain it to us?" And, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.240
really couldn't explain it. What, what do you say?&nbsp;
I mean, how do you explain something like that?

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:23.340
SIERRA: Why It Matters spends a lot of time&nbsp;
discussing how things that happen around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:30.240
world affect us at home. But today we&nbsp;
are going to flip that around. Because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.760
the killing of George Floyd, the protests&nbsp;
against police brutality and systemic racism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.400
and the administration’s response, are not&nbsp;
only unfolding here, in the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:47.340
The world is listening, too. And depending on&nbsp;
where you are, the echoes can sound different.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.280
To better understand how this is playing out, we&nbsp;
turned to two American journalists who have spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.960
their careers reporting abroad. We asked them&nbsp;
to give us their own thoughts and experiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.100
and to describe what America looks&nbsp;
like, right now, through the eyes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:07.860
those who are watching from afar. They told&nbsp;
us about two places; Africa, and Hong Kong.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.760
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It&nbsp;
Matters. Today, diplomacy starts at home.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.000
RICHBURG: This kind of reminds me&nbsp;
how, throughout history, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.580
and I've studied history and political science,&nbsp;
throughout history, America's goal and mission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.720
of trying to go around to promote democracy and&nbsp;
human rights around the world has constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.220
been undercut by how they treat minorities&nbsp;
and particularly African Americans at home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:41.160
I'm Keith Richburg, I'm currently&nbsp;
the Director of the Journalism and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.720
Media Studies Center at The University of&nbsp;
Hong Kong, but I spent most of my career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.740
about 34 years as a reporter, and&nbsp;
correspondent for The Washington Post.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.580
During the Cold War, the old Soviet Union&nbsp;
propagandists used to take great pleasure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:01.020
in pointing out how Black Americans were treated&nbsp;
in the American South during the Jim Crow era.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:05.760
You know, the propagandists during the Vietnam&nbsp;
War would like to point out how American Blacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:11.220
were being treated. You know, we were not able&nbsp;
to really, as strongly as we wanted to, stand up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:16.020
against the Apartheid regime in South Africa in&nbsp;
its early days, because the Apartheid regime in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:22.380
South Africa was in many ways modeled on the Jim&nbsp;
Crow segregation laws of the American South. So I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:27.960
think throughout history, America's stated mission&nbsp;
and goal of promoting democracy and human rights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:32.820
and Roosevelt's four freedoms around the world&nbsp;
have constantly been undercut by enemies who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:37.860
willing to point out the hypocrisy of America's&nbsp;
positions and America's promotion of human rights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:41.700
by saying, "Before you come lecture us, why&nbsp;
don't you deal with your own problems at home?”.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:51.480
ODUAH: It's quite painful for me because I do have&nbsp;
both experiences, you know, living in America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:57.120
born in Nigeria, I do have both views and&nbsp;
I've experienced this racism in America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:05.040
My name is Chika Oduah. I am an independent&nbsp;
multimedia journalist. I am based in West Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.480
and I cover the entire continent for&nbsp;
various international media outlets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:15.420
The killing of George Floyd was very&nbsp;
jarring. It was a wake up. It was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:22.800
brutal awakening for people who actually&nbsp;
don't quite believe racism is as real as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.060
African Americans say that it is. You’ve still&nbsp;
got people who don't believe it's that strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:32.220
who believe that it's from the past, that&nbsp;
there have been many, moves towards overcoming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:37.740
you know, some people still believe that America&nbsp;
is a post racial society. So for this incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.820
this killing of George Floyd it’s like, "Oh,&nbsp;
actually, it's still there, you know, and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:47.760
saw it on TV. We saw this guy breathing for his&nbsp;
life for nearly nine minutes." So in that aspect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:53.760
it is a rude awakening. It's really provoking&nbsp;
some nations to look inward, you know, look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:59.160
their own injustice. For example, in France, you&nbsp;
know, people there were calling for an end to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:04.800
choke hold that some police officers use. And so&nbsp;
they're having debates on how to handle people.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:11.640
RICHBURG: You know, I was overseas, when the&nbsp;
Rodney King episode happened in Los Angeles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:16.620
1992, and you started to see riots&nbsp;
going on. I was in Nairobi, Kenya,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:20.820
and while the U.S. was criticizing the Kenyan&nbsp;
government for using the police to violently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:25.620
break up pro-democracy demonstrations in Kenya,&nbsp;
they could easily point back to the U.S. and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:29.520
"Well, why don't you deal with your own situation&nbsp;
before you come and lecture us." I have to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:35.220
as a Black reporter overseas, I've constantly&nbsp;
heard it said to me by officials, whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:39.240
they'd be from China, or in the Middle East,&nbsp;
or elsewhere, saying, "Well, hold on. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:43.740
Black. So you understand what I'm talking about.&nbsp;
You know how bad the U.S. is. You know how bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:48.060
the U.S. government is." As if I'm supposed to&nbsp;
kind of be able to take sides with, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:51.840
an oppressive government because I'm supposed to&nbsp;
know that the U.S. government is also oppressive.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.340
SIERRA:&nbsp;

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:57.120
So, what's it like being an&nbsp;
American in Hong Kong right now?

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.800
RICHBURG: Well, it's, it's really interesting.&nbsp;
You've got a lot of things going on here at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:06.060
the same time. We've been dealing with one&nbsp;
year since the massive protests started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:12.000
since the now pulled Extradition Bill, and it's&nbsp;
been a year of turmoil really. I mean, last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.040
2019, almost every weekend was a different protest&nbsp;
or demonstration in the streets. Some of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.900
incredibly violent with police on both&nbsp;
sides, police violence, protester violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:27.780
vandalism. Uh, it kind of culminated in November&nbsp;
11th when the city just kind of exploded.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.870
South China Morning Post: Hong Kong Protest.

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:44.880
RICHBURG: The universities were taken over by&nbsp;
students who were battling police, and now I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.220
watching the U.S. and while Hong Kong is, uh,&nbsp;
relatively quiet, the U.S. seems to have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:54.660
protest movement that's kind of only growing,&nbsp;
and becoming more sustained, so it's kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.660 --> 00:07:03.300
interesting now to watch, you know, the U.S. going&nbsp;
through what we went through. And the other major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:08.040
thing that's happened is the Chinese government&nbsp;
in Beijing has announced it's going to impose a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:13.380
new national security law on Hong Kong. This is&nbsp;
the thing that President Trump said is really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:17.880
kind of stripping away Hong Kong's autonomy. But&nbsp;
from the Chinese perspective, it gives the police,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:23.340
and the Chinese government new tools to be able&nbsp;
to control, the unrest that we've been seeing. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.820
I think that you, you can basically call&nbsp;
it a crackdown coming from the north.

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:33.480
SIERRA: So you brought up President Trump.&nbsp;
A lot of people are saying that the way the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:38.700
Trump administration reacted to anti-racism&nbsp;
protests, following the killing of George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:44.660
Floyd has undercut our moral authority&nbsp;
abroad. Do you feel like this is the case?

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:50.400
RICHBURG: I think it's definitely the case that&nbsp;
the, the Trump administration's reaction to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:56.400
protests, and the actual protests themselves have&nbsp;
totally undercut America's kind of moral standing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.480
and ability to chastise others abroad for&nbsp;
how they deal with demonstrators. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:05.280
here in Hong Kong, we saw very violent&nbsp;
police reaction to peaceful protests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:10.380
that started in June 9th of 2019. Uh, the&nbsp;
police used tear gas, they've used rubber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:15.060
bullets. They've used live bullets in a&nbsp;
few occasions. I've seen them beating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:19.140
you know, handcuffed, and perfectly subdued&nbsp;
protestors, who were not resisting arrest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:24.720
real incidents of police brutality. I've seen them&nbsp;
covering up their badges, and their identifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.620
numbers with black tape, so we, there's no way&nbsp;
you could actually report who was doing what.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:28.980
SIERRA: Wow.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:32.700
RICHBURG: All of this has been captured on&nbsp;
video, and the administration through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:37.080
state department, and through the U.S. consulate&nbsp;
here has consistently condemned these kind of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:42.540
violent action against peaceful protestors,&nbsp;
but now we see police in the U.S. doing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:46.500
same things, it's really kind of like&nbsp;
watching an echo going on now, so yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:50.280
it's really sapped I think the moral authority&nbsp;
of the U.S. and U.S. officials to kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:54.120
criticize what's going on, you know, here&nbsp;
in Hong Kong, and elsewhere in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:02.580
For example, there was a really, biting cartoon&nbsp;
in China Daily, which is one of the communist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:08.640
owned party English language newspapers showing&nbsp;
a policeman wearing a KU Klux Klan hood with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:13.260
his knee on the neck of the Statue of Liberty.&nbsp;
And the, and the cartoon was labeled, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:18.180
“racism”. It's just the sort of thing that&nbsp;
is almost a godsend to the Beijing government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:22.740
propagandists, to say, "You see, you see what&nbsp;
we're dealing with here? Look at the hypocrisy."

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:27.420
SIERRA: How is the African media&nbsp;
covering the killing of George Floyd?

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:33.180
ODUAH: I think the African community, both within&nbsp;
the United States and back on the continent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:40.440
there's a lot of intrigue about this particular&nbsp;
killing. And the media has also played a big role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:45.840
in trying to educate people in Africa about&nbsp;
what is going on. There's a lot of questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:51.360
There's some general assumptions that racism&nbsp;
is the order of the day, but you do have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:58.260
media trying to explain it. So in this case,&nbsp;
Africans like myself were called upon as foreign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:03.300
correspondents to explain what's happening, the,&nbsp;
the turmoil that we see in the United States.

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:06.900
SIERRA: And what sort of reactions&nbsp;
are you seeing to the protests?

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:12.780
ODUAH: There's a lot of sympathy. You know,&nbsp;
we're seeing overwhelming solidarity for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:17.940
demand for justice, and I think that this&nbsp;
is a page turner. It's, it's not something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:24.300
that we always see with Africans. Sometimes,&nbsp;
there's some blame, towards the African American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:28.920
community for whatever reason, but now there&nbsp;
seems to be a lot of sympathy and solidarity.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:35.700
SIERRA: What kinds of opinions were you&nbsp;
hearing about black Americans in Africa before?

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:42.540
ODUAH: That's a really good question. I mean, for&nbsp;
example, in Nigeria, there are diverse opinions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:49.140
to racism in America. I've met some Nigerians&nbsp;
who just don't understand it. And the reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:53.940
why Nigerians typically don't quite understand&nbsp;
racism is because they've never experienced it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.940 --> 00:11:00.840
firsthand. Nigeria was not a settler colony. I&nbsp;
mean, you know, it was a British colony, sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:07.080
but you didn't see large populations of British&nbsp;
people or Europeans living in Nigeria. So for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:12.480
most part, Nigerians have kind of run their state&nbsp;
of affairs. They've never had large numbers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:19.020
British people in their societies. So they don't&nbsp;
quite understand racism in the way that African&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:26.040
Americans have experienced it. I've come across&nbsp;
people who say African Americans are threatening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:31.260
that maybe they are criminals, that they're at&nbsp;
the wrong place at the wrong time. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:38.820
things like that. A lot of prejudices that really&nbsp;
speak to gaps in the black experience. I mean the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:43.560
black experience is not one single experience.&nbsp;
And for a lot of people on the continent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:50.820
they don't quite share what African Americans&nbsp;
experience. If you go to Southern Africa, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:58.620
know, like South Africa, Zimbabwe, Zambia, these&nbsp;
countries did experience large populations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:05.880
Europeans in their communities. These were what we&nbsp;
call settler colonies. So they did experience, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:12.660
know, having white people govern them. So in those&nbsp;
places, they have a better understanding of racism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:19.560
in America because they experienced apartheid,&nbsp;
which was legalized racism in their society.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:28.440
Africans also have a stake in what's happening in&nbsp;
terms of racism and police brutality in America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:34.200
because Africans have also been affected. I mean,&nbsp;
some of us know the case of Amadou Diallo in New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:40.740
York who was shot simply because he reached&nbsp;
into his pocket trying to get his wallet. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:46.860
that happened in 1999. There have been other&nbsp;
cases of Africans shot by police in America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:51.780
So we've gotta see this I'm saying, we,&nbsp;
because I'm African as well, we have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:57.660
see that we're also invested in American&nbsp;
society. Many of us come here for education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:03.300
we come here for economic opportunities, we come&nbsp;
here to get houses, things like that. We can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:08.760
targeted, you know. Oftentimes Africans&nbsp;
like to see themselves as different from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:12.780
African Americans when they come here and&nbsp;
say, "Oh, we're different. We're not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:17.760
same. We have different histories." But&nbsp;
to a racist, we are the same, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:22.140
if you just look at us on the street, we're the&nbsp;
same. And I'd like for Africans to remember that.

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:28.320
SIERRA: Do you think that these&nbsp;
mass protests that we're having in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:33.480
the U.S. have caused observers abroad&nbsp;
to draw stronger distinctions between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:37.920
the values of American people and the&nbsp;
values of the current administration?

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:43.980
ODUAH: You know, many Africans are studying&nbsp;
the American political landscape. They like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:49.380
to see what's going on. I've got relatives in&nbsp;
Nigeria who follow it so closely that you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:53.640
that they're living in America, but they're&nbsp;
not. They just follow it so closely because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:58.860
they aspire to be like America. They&nbsp;
want their societies to be democratic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:07.260
to be emblems of unity and free thinking and&nbsp;
progressive politics. But now, you know, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:12.840
think that view of America as a beacon of hope and&nbsp;
democracy has been tinted and they are somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:18.480
disillusioned by this current administration and&nbsp;
they're making comments to reflect their thoughts.

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:26.220
SIERRA: What sentiments have you encountered&nbsp;
in your reporting about the upcoming election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:32.580
in the U.S.? You know, what do you think changes&nbsp;
for Africa when the U.S. administration changes?

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:39.660
ODUAH: The views are so diverse. You know, I would&nbsp;
say in the likes of many West African countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:45.180
some people do want to see Trump remain&nbsp;
because they believe that Trump will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:53.460
help to fight against terrorism. You know, the&nbsp;
likes of Mali, Burkina Faso, Chad and Nigeria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:59.100
which are seeing, you know, heightened acts&nbsp;
of terrorism by several terrorist groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:02.760
not just one. They want to see Trump because&nbsp;
they feel like he's doing a good job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:08.340
or he will try to make greater efforts and trying&nbsp;
to fight that and combat that with, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:14.340
U.S. soldiers on [the] ground. So in that aspect,&nbsp;
some people would like to see him remain. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:19.260
others, you know, they don't think that he's&nbsp;
for Africa. They don't see him trying to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:24.900
economic ties with Africa. America is really&nbsp;
losing out. We're seeing China, we're seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:30.180
Russia coming in. We're seeing Germany coming&nbsp;
in trying to forge and strengthen economic ties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:35.340
but America not so much. Trump has been, you&nbsp;
know, quiet on that aspect. I don't think he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:40.680
really sees Africa as an opportunity for him&nbsp;
economically or, or politically, I mean, at all.

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:47.400
RICHBURG: Before the protesters in Hong Kong were&nbsp;
calling on the U.S. government for help. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:51.660
the things that I found most striking about the&nbsp;
last year of protest in Hong Kong were the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:56.880
of American flags I saw the protestors flying or&nbsp;
displaying in the middle of protest. They were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:01.740
calling on the U.S. Congress to implement the Hong&nbsp;
Kong Democracy Act. They were calling on the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:06.660
to take away Hong Kong’s special status. They were&nbsp;
marching in peaceful numbers to the U.S. Consulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:11.160
here in Hong Kong, and begging the U.S Consulate&nbsp;
to do something, to help promote democracy in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:17.160
Hong Kong, and so now it's the Chinese government,&nbsp;
and the pro-Beijing side that's basically saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:20.220
“Look, look at what the Americans are doing to&nbsp;
their own people. You think they're going to come,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:24.840
and help you here in Hong Kong?” Of course,&nbsp;
racism and injustice persist. But I always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:28.140
try to tell people, look, America is a&nbsp;
very big and a very complicated place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:33.060
and please do not lose sight of the fact that&nbsp;
we did elect a black man as president. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:37.980
we elected him twice. So it wasn't a fluke.&nbsp;
We have now more black elected officials than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:42.780
probably at any time in history. We have more&nbsp;
black CEOs, we have more black journalists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:48.420
we have more black millionaires, we have more&nbsp;
black judges. You still have problems of racial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:53.880
injustice and racial inequality, but at the same&nbsp;
time, America is constantly making progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:58.740
and it's a country that constantly takes in&nbsp;
my view, two steps forward, and one step back.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:04.860
SIERRA: What do you think the&nbsp;
reaction has been from mainland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:07.500
Chinese citizens? How are they seeing this moment?

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:13.320
RICHBURG: Well, it's a good question. You know,&nbsp;
and I, it's hard to actually speak for mainland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:18.180
China because it's a huge country. It's 1.4&nbsp;
billion people, diverse. I think you're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:24.180
to see some drawback of Chinese, students, going&nbsp;
to the United States to study or Chinese wanting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:29.400
to immigrate to the United States. Anytime there&nbsp;
is a story in the United States or Canada or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:35.160
Australia of racism directed against Asians -&nbsp;
and we've seen many ugly incidents of racism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:40.500
Those stories are front page news in China.&nbsp;
Those stories are front page news in Hong Kong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:45.060
because there's a message the Chinese government&nbsp;
is trying to send out, which is, "You see how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:49.080
bad it is there? You're going to be discriminated&nbsp;
against there. You're better off back here because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.080 --> 00:17:53.760
look how racist they are in America." Now, the&nbsp;
irony is that China can be just as racist and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:58.680
Chinese can be just as racist as we saw when they&nbsp;
were discriminating against blacks and Africans in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:03.780
Guangzhou, basically accusing blacks and Africans&nbsp;
in Guangzhou and Southern China of bringing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:07.500
coronavirus. There were literally signs&nbsp;
in McDonalds saying, "We don't serve black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:13.680
people." So China can be just as racist, but they&nbsp;
love picking up these stories because it feeds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:19.020
their larger narrative that the west is not some&nbsp;
panacea, that Chinese should stay home in China.

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:23.940
SIERRA: So switching gears a&nbsp;
little bit to your own experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:29.580
what insights do you have about the treatment&nbsp;
of the press in the U.S. during these protests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:32.760
and, you know, over the last few&nbsp;
years by this administration?

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:37.620
ODUAH: You know, it really reminds me of what&nbsp;
happens in Africa. Seeing the president of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:41.700
United States call the National Guard,&nbsp;
tear gassing journalists, tear gassing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:47.280
peaceful protesters, you know, it just&nbsp;
makes me think of authoritarian regimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:52.260
in Africa. The likes of Rwanda, where media&nbsp;
has been suppressed, the likes of Nigeria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:59.100
many countries we're seeing this. Ethiopia,&nbsp;
Eritrea, journalists are not wanted, and those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:04.140
administrations will do anything that they can&nbsp;
to stop the media from telling the story. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:09.420
for me, this is just like, a lot of people&nbsp;
like to describe Trump as an African president.

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:15.120
RICHBURG: I can't count how many statements&nbsp;
the Foreign Correspondents Club, or the FCC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:19.920
has put out condemning the use of tear gas&nbsp;
against journalists, the use of pepper spray,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:25.500
journalists shot by rubber bullets. Here in Hong&nbsp;
Kong, this Indonesian female journalist was shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:31.080
in the eye with a rubber bullet, and was blinded.&nbsp;
The police still have not resolved that case. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:36.060
then I was just kind of almost stunned to see that&nbsp;
there was an American photographer who was shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:40.260
in the eye with a rubber bullet, and similarly&nbsp;
blinded in one eye, and it's almost like I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:45.300
watching what happened here in Hong Kong unfold&nbsp;
in slow motion again in The United States. I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:49.440
seen journalists pepper sprayed, I was watching&nbsp;
a journalist, a female reporter in Louisville,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:54.780
Kentucky, I believe it was, who was hit by rubber&nbsp;
bullets while she was on the air live. I've seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:59.700
journalists hit with batons. It's happened to&nbsp;
journalists from all over the world. It’s really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:05.040
been disheartening because we first saw this echo&nbsp;
from The United States and from the White House,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:09.240
coming here, when President Trump started&nbsp;
using this phrase “fake news”. Everything in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.240 --> 00:20:14.100
the U.S. is fake news, and that was picked up by&nbsp;
dictators and autocrats in this part of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:21.120
from Hun Sen in Cambodia, to the military regime&nbsp;
in Myanmar, which tried to cover up their massacre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:26.400
of the Rohingya Muslims by calling it all fake&nbsp;
news the Chinese government calls anything it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:31.440
doesn't like fake news, the Thai military will&nbsp;
call anything fake news. So we've seen now this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:36.960
echo of Trump coming out here in this direction.&nbsp;
And then you see this kind of brutal activity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:42.120
the police now, and I fear that that's going to&nbsp;
become a second case, where people will think now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:46.200
in this part of the world, that they can get&nbsp;
away with this kind of treatment of journalists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:49.020
firing rubber bullets at them, using&nbsp;
tear gas, because they're gonna say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:52.200
"Hold on a second. If you're doing it in the&nbsp;
U.S., how can you criticize us for doing it?"

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:56.640
SIERRA: It's the kind of trend setting&nbsp;
you don't wanna be, you know, setting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.640 --> 00:21:01.080
making the jobs of authoritarian&nbsp;
leaders easier around the world.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:05.100
RICHBURG: Yeah. It's not the trend we wanna&nbsp;
be setting. I believe Richard Haass, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:08.880
the President of the Council on Foreign Relations,&nbsp;
I believe he always talks about how foreign policy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:14.460
begins at home, and I really do believe that's&nbsp;
true. You know, our greatest asset in our foreign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:19.800
policy is our own behavior at home. And if we&nbsp;
could set an example that here's how we deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:23.700
with peaceful demonstrations in the U.S.,&nbsp;
here's how we respect journalistic freedom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:28.380
here's how we respect freedom of the press, here's&nbsp;
how we treat our minorities, here's how we treat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:33.900
the Black population that's facing injustice.&nbsp;
If we could show, by the power of our example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:41.220
how we do things I think we can regain that moral&nbsp;
authority. I was a foreign correspondent in 2009&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.220 --> 00:21:47.880
after Obama was elected. And it was amazing how&nbsp;
transformative the election of Barack Obama was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:52.140
I was overseas. I was based in Europe. I was&nbsp;
a Paris correspondent for the Washington Post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:57.360
when the United States invaded Iraq. And I&nbsp;
thought this would take forever to repair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:03.480
America's image after the disastrous war in Iraq.&nbsp;
But then the moment you had the election in 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:07.380
when Obama was elected, and I came back into&nbsp;
Asia. And I would walk through airports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:12.960
whether it was Beijing or Jakarta or Bangkok, and&nbsp;
I kid you not, uh, immigration officers at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:16.440
airports would look at my passport, see an, a see&nbsp;
an American, they'd look up and see a black man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:21.480
and they would, they would give me the thumbs up&nbsp;
and have big smile, because what we do in the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:26.520
does make a difference around the world. It does&nbsp;
permeate around the world. And I hope it can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:30.000
seen. I mean, I'm not going to predict anything.&nbsp;
We all were looking at the polls, but I hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:35.460
this era we're in now is eventually seen as an&nbsp;
aberration and not who the Americans really are.

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:42.960
ODUAH: I think that this is a great&nbsp;
opportunity for Africans on the continent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:48.480
both in terms of citizens and government,&nbsp;
to really stand in solidarity with African&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:55.020
Americans. I think that this is a great chance&nbsp;
to bridge that long standing gap. There have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.020 --> 00:23:01.140
been some attempts to connect, you know, too&nbsp;
black people across the pond, but largely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:07.560
those efforts have been weak. You know, we did&nbsp;
see the likes of Malcolm X, for example, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:14.940
Maya Angelou, and Martin Luther King. These black&nbsp;
figures did go to Africa and they were inspired.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:22.380
You’ve also got cases of African heroes who&nbsp;
did go to United States, seeking inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.380 --> 00:23:28.620
and went back to their countries to fuel&nbsp;
movements, independence movements. I’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:34.260
like to see more of that. I’d like to see more&nbsp;
comradery, more collaboration, more dialogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:38.880
between African Americans and Africa. So I think&nbsp;
this is a great opportunity to see that happen.

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:46.920
ODUAH: I mean, growing up here, I remember being&nbsp;
targeted for my skin color, being targeted for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:52.320
being Nigerian, for being African. So I kind of&nbsp;
have a holistic grasp on the entire, uh, on all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:58.380
of these racist agendas and sentiments. So for me,&nbsp;
it's quite heartbreaking. There are times where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:04.740
I've had to explain and justify my right to exist&nbsp;
in America. And I lived in the South, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.740 --> 00:24:10.140
Atlanta, Georgia, and you see a lot of deep&nbsp;
racism in the South. It's still there. Some of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:16.320
classmates were racist. Some of my neighbors were&nbsp;
racist. And once they find out that we're not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:21.420
black, but we're African they're even angrier, you&nbsp;
know, some people. So for me, it's very personal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.420 --> 00:24:27.840
So I do find myself crying when I hear stories&nbsp;
like George Floyd and Amadou Diallo and Breonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:34.440
Taylor and Ahmaud Arbery. It really disturbs my&nbsp;
soul to be honest. And so for me, I definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:40.740
identify with the black experience, the diversity&nbsp;
of black experiences, and that experience does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.740 --> 00:24:48.360
come with persecution, with being targeted.&nbsp;
So I like to speak as a voice for both African&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:55.500
Americans and Africans. And like I said earlier, I&nbsp;
would love to see more solidarity between African&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.500 --> 00:25:00.780
Americans and Africans, because these groups&nbsp;
have historically misunderstood each other.

00:25:02.660 --> 00:25:08.040
RICHBURG: One of my earliest and most vivid&nbsp;
childhood memories was being nine years old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:12.720
when the city of Detroit experienced, what was&nbsp;
then one of the worst riots in American history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:18.660
It was a riot that had its roots in a lot of&nbsp;
economic and social issues, but primarily in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:24.540
the brutality of a primarily white police force in&nbsp;
the city of Detroit. And it ended up with about 43&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.540 --> 00:25:29.160
people dead, I think, by the count of the Detroit&nbsp;
Free Press and the other papers. Most of them were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:35.040
people who were shot by police or national&nbsp;
guardsmen. I remember my father taking me as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:40.140
a nine year old kid, you know, me and my brother&nbsp;
to go watch the burning down of the shop, little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.140 --> 00:25:44.940
strip mall where we used to go buy our comic books&nbsp;
and buy our candy at the Kresge's. And you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:48.060
why would you take little kids there? He said,&nbsp;
"I want you to see this. I want you to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:52.980
what these people are doing to their own city.&nbsp;
There's no point in rioting." The 1967 riots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:58.440
it perpetuated a lot of change. It eventually&nbsp;
led to the election in 1973 of Coleman Young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.440 --> 00:26:03.480
the first black mayor of Detroit. It led to the&nbsp;
integration of the police in Detroit. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.080 --> 00:26:09.180
out of bad things can come something good and&nbsp;
out of the Detroit riots, you did get this kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:15.120
of reform. I would like to think that out of&nbsp;
these protests over the death of George Floyd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:19.440
we might get something good. We maybe will&nbsp;
get real police reform, that people have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:24.300
been talking about for a while. So I, that's what&nbsp;
I'm really hopeful for. So maybe everything that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:29.160
I saw in 1967 is kind of coming to a head now&nbsp;
and it just, you know, it just took 53 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:33.000
That's all. I mean, we get ... we eventually get&nbsp;
there. It takes us a while, but we do get there.

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:39.960
9 News Australia:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.960 --> 00:26:46.440
This is not something that only happens in the&nbsp;
U.S., it’s something that is happening globally.&nbsp;

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:49.969
Bloomberg: Everyone keeps saying “Oh, Australia&nbsp;
is so different”, but it’s not really.

00:26:49.969 --> 00:26:54.528
France 24: We need to take a deep&nbsp;
look into ourselves as French people.

00:26:54.528 --> 00:26:58.200
Al Jazeera: People think the UK is innocent, the&nbsp;
UK is not innocent. It happens here everyday.

00:26:59.880 --> 00:27:05.280
France 24: We are sick and tired of the police&nbsp;
brutality. We want justice for everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:08.880
regardless of in Kenya or all over&nbsp;
the world. Nobody needs to be killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:10.009
nobody needs to be beaten.

00:27:10.009 --> 00:27:16.620
France 24: Black lives matter. And it doesn’t mean&nbsp;
that other lives do not matter. There is only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:22.920
house burning, and we’ll all pay attention&nbsp;
to that house. It doesn’t mean other houses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:29.100
are not important, they too are important.&nbsp;
But this house is the one that is burning.

